From: Rob Vogelaar <revogelaar@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] orch: serialize candidate completion with command lifetime

A fresh 20-client room acceptance run aborted in orch_transient after its
command statistics disappeared. Candidate responses and all-rejected ACKs
complete commands on radio threads while the manager timer traverses the
same active queue, so completion could free both the command and its stats
between update_stats and orch_transient.

Protect queue/stat operations with a dedicated recursive command mutex.
Hold the same guard throughout response correlation, ACK command access,
result publication and synchronous completion, and during controller radio
timer command access. This preserves immediate next-query admission without
reintroducing the deferred-completion HTTP 504 race fixed by patch 0076.

Nested orchestration helpers and timeout cancellation require recursive
locking. Do not hold the command mutex across submit_commands preprocessing:
reset/delete operations stop radio threads there. EM-map traversal is nested
inside command locking, never the reverse.

The compiled real-method regression deterministically reproduces early
destruction before this patch and verifies both lock directions, nested
completion, duplicate completion and immediate follow-up submission.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---

diff --git a/inc/em_orch.h b/inc/em_orch.h
--- a/inc/em_orch.h
+++ b/inc/em_orch.h
@@ -21,13 +21,21 @@
 
 #include "em_base.h"
 #include "em.h"
+#include <mutex>
 
 class em_cmd_t;
 class em_mgr_t;
 
 class em_orch_t {
 
+    std::recursive_mutex m_command_mutex;
+
 public:
+    std::unique_lock<std::recursive_mutex> lock_commands()
+    {
+        return std::unique_lock<std::recursive_mutex>(m_command_mutex);
+    }
+
     em_mgr_t    *m_mgr;
     queue_t *m_pending;
     queue_t *m_active;
